---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BmiCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/BmiCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="bmi-calculator">
  <BmiCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to calculate your BMI</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick metric or imperial with the unit toggle. This device remembers your choice.</li>
      <li>Enter your height. In imperial mode there are separate feet and inches boxes.</li>
      <li>Enter your weight. The conversions show below the box, so you can check them.</li>
      <li>
        Read the gauge. The needle points to your score, the highlighted arc is your category, and the
        healthy weight range for your exact height is next to it.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The BMI calculator runs in JavaScript on this page, so the numbers you type stay in your browser.
    </p>

    <h2>The BMI formula</h2>
    <p>
      Body mass index is weight divided by height squared. In metric units, that is kilograms divided
      by metres squared:
    </p>
    <p><strong>BMI = kg ÷ m²</strong></p>
    <p>
      In imperial units, the same relation needs a conversion factor of 703, because pounds and inches
      are not SI units:
    </p>
    <p><strong>BMI = 703 × lb ÷ in²</strong></p>
    <p>
      An adult of 1.75 m and 72 kg has a BMI of 72 ÷ (1.75 × 1.75) = 72 ÷ 3.0625 = 23.5. In imperial, the
      same person is 5 ft 9 in (69 in) and 158.7 lb: 703 × 158.7 ÷ 4,761 = 23.4. The small difference
      comes from rounding.
    </p>

    <h2>BMI categories for adults</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Category</th>
            <th scope="col">BMI range</th>
            <th scope="col">What it generally indicates</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Underweight</td>
            <td>Below 18.5</td>
            <td>Higher risk of nutrient deficiency, reduced bone density and impaired immunity</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Healthy weight</td>
            <td>18.5–24.9</td>
            <td>The band with the lowest all-cause mortality in large population studies</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Overweight</td>
            <td>25.0–29.9</td>
            <td>Slightly raised cardiometabolic risk on average, but strongly confounded by muscle mass</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Obesity class I</td>
            <td>30.0–34.9</td>
            <td>Clearly raised risk of type 2 diabetes, hypertension and joint problems</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Obesity class II</td>
            <td>35.0–39.9</td>
            <td>Substantially raised risk; medical support usually improves outcomes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Obesity class III</td>
            <td>40.0 and above</td>
            <td>Highest statistical risk band, sometimes called severe obesity</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      These cut-offs come from the World Health Organization and apply to adults of any sex aged 20
      and over. Several Asian countries use lower thresholds (23 for overweight and 27.5 for obesity),
      because cardiometabolic risk rises at a lower BMI in those populations.
    </p>

    <h2>Healthy weight ranges by height</h2>
    <p>
      The range below is BMI 18.5 to 24.9 converted back into weight. It is wide: at 1.75 m, the bottom
      and top of "healthy" are more than 20 kg apart.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Height</th>
            <th scope="col">Healthy weight (metric)</th>
            <th scope="col">Healthy weight (imperial)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1.55 m · 5 ft 1 in</td><td>44.4–59.8 kg</td><td>98–132 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.60 m · 5 ft 3 in</td><td>47.4–63.7 kg</td><td>104–140 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.65 m · 5 ft 5 in</td><td>50.4–67.8 kg</td><td>111–149 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.70 m · 5 ft 7 in</td><td>53.5–72.0 kg</td><td>118–159 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.75 m · 5 ft 9 in</td><td>56.7–76.3 kg</td><td>125–168 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.80 m · 5 ft 11 in</td><td>59.9–80.7 kg</td><td>132–178 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.85 m · 6 ft 1 in</td><td>63.3–85.2 kg</td><td>140–188 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.90 m · 6 ft 3 in</td><td>66.8–89.9 kg</td><td>147–198 lb</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Where BMI breaks down</h2>
    <p>
      Adolphe Quetelet devised BMI in the 1830s to describe populations, not individuals, and it has
      only two inputs. It is cheap, reproducible and easy to compare across studies, but it cannot see
      most of what matters clinically.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Muscle reads as fat. Muscle is denser than adipose tissue, so trained athletes often fall in
        the "overweight" band with single-digit body fat. On BMI alone, most professional rugby players
        and sprinters are obese.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fat distribution is invisible. Visceral fat around the organs has much more metabolic risk than
        subcutaneous fat on the hips and thighs, but BMI cannot tell them apart. A waist measurement
        can.
      </li>
      <li>
        It is height-biased. For similar body shapes, weight scales closer to height cubed than height
        squared, so BMI reads slightly high for tall people and low for short people. The Ponderal
        index in the results uses height cubed and is more stable at the extremes.
      </li>
      <li>
        Age and ethnicity change the meaning. Older adults have less muscle and more fat at the same
        BMI, and health risk appears at different thresholds in different populations.
      </li>
      <li>
        It is not valid for everyone. Children, pregnant people, and people with significant oedema or
        an amputation need different tools.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Better checks to run alongside BMI</h2>
    <p>
      If your BMI is near a boundary, two more numbers tell you much more than a second decimal place.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Waist-to-height ratio: measure your waist at the navel and divide by your height in the same
        units. Below 0.5 is the low-risk zone. It works across heights, sexes and ethnic groups better
        than BMI.
      </li>
      <li>
        Body fat percentage: a tape measure and the US Navy equation give a usable estimate in two
        minutes, and it separates muscular people from heavy people.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Both calculators are on this site and run on your device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
